.TH TALK 1 "August 2026" "Bashy" "POSIX Commands"
.SH NAME
talk \- exchange text with another logged-in user on the local host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B talk
.I address
.RI [ terminal ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B talk
opens an interactive, screen-oriented conversation with another operating-system
account logged in on the same host.  The recipient must have an eligible terminal
and must permit terminal messages.  If more than one eligible terminal exists,
the optional
.I terminal
operand selects one.
.PP
Bashy implements the host-local behavior required by POSIX.  Host-qualified and
remote addresses are rejected.  No talk daemon is contacted.  Invitations are
written to the recipient terminal and conversation events travel over private,
authenticated, ephemeral local-domain sockets; no transcript is retained.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I address
The name of a local operating-system account with an active terminal session.
.TP
.I terminal
An optional terminal name, with or without the
.I /dev/
prefix, belonging to that account.
.SH TERMINAL OPERATION
Both standard input and standard output must be terminals.  The terminal type
must provide clear-screen, cursor-addressing, and erase-line capabilities and a
display of at least 20 columns by 6 rows.  During the conversation the terminal
is placed temporarily into non-canonical, no-echo mode; its original settings
are restored on every normal and error exit.
.PP
The display maintains separately labelled local and peer regions.  The
configured erase, kill, interrupt, and end-of-file characters keep their terminal
meanings.  An alert event rings the terminal bell.  Peer closure does not discard
local input; the local participant chooses when to exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B TERM
selects terminal capabilities.  Locale variables affect character decoding,
display width, and diagnostics.  The Bashy-specific
.B BASHY_TALK_DIR
may select the root-owned sticky directory used for local session endpoints.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/run/bashy/talk
Default shared endpoint directory when available.  It must be root-owned and
have sticky world-searchable semantics; unsafe ownership or mode is rejected.
.TP
.I /var/run/utmp
Login-session data used to locate the recipient terminal on systems that provide
it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates a conversation terminated normally, including the configured EOF
or interrupt character or SIGINT.  Greater than zero indicates invalid syntax,
an unknown or unavailable recipient, denied terminal messages, inadequate
terminal capabilities, unsafe local IPC state, authentication failure, or an
input/output or cleanup error.
.SH SECURITY
Endpoint metadata is short-lived and owner-checked.  Conversation datagrams are
authenticated and encrypted; malformed, legacy, injected, or over-sized events
are rejected.  The implementation never contacts another host.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mesg (1),
.BR who (1),
.BR write (1)
.SH HISTORY
The talk command appeared in 4.2BSD.  Bashy's local-to-local behavior follows the
loopback-safe model used by later BSD systems while replacing talkd with private
local IPC.
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2008, 2016 Edition (Issue 7).
